---
import HarnessScene from './HarnessScene.astro';
import '../styles/harness-walkthrough.css';

const chapters = [
  {
    id: 'the-first-run',
    phase: 'The problem',
    label: 'The first run',
    title: 'A draft is only the first step',
    scene: 'draft',
    body: [
      'Ask an agent to research Northwind and write a follow-up. With a model, a prompt, and a search tool, it can produce a useful draft in one session.',
      'Now give the same job to a team. Someone who knows the domain has to decide what counts as a qualified prospect, which record the team should trust, and what to do when the evidence disagrees.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'where-the-work-breaks',
    phase: 'Why it gets harder',
    label: 'Scattered state',
    title: 'The work gets scattered across tools',
    scene: 'fragments',
    body: [
      'The researcher calls Northwind qualified in chat. The CRM has two records with similar names. The outreach draft is in someone’s inbox. A second agent can see more data and still choose the wrong company.',
      'The team needs a shared understanding of what these records mean, where to read and update them, and which decisions need someone with domain expertise.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'how-it-works',
    phase: 'Map the domain',
    label: 'Inventory the apps',
    title: 'Start with the apps people use',
    scene: 'landscape',
    legacy: 'how-does-a-persona-work',
    body: [
      'Work with a domain specialist to list the apps the organization uses. For each one, record who uses it, what data it owns, what the team can read or update, and how to connect it. The inventory shows how many apps are involved and where the work crosses between them.',
      'For Lane’s prospecting work, start with Notion for prospect records, Gmail for outreach, and Google Calendar for meetings. The specialist confirms which app is authoritative for each record.',
    ],
    link: { href: './concepts/personas-and-workspaces/#map-the-organizations-apps', text: 'Map the organization’s apps' },
  },
  {
    id: 'map-the-ontology',
    phase: 'Map the domain',
    label: 'Define the ontology',
    title: 'Give the apps a shared vocabulary',
    scene: 'persona',
    body: [
      'Use the inventory to define the ontology. A prospect can have outreach messages and meetings. The ontology gives those entities stable names, fields, and relationships. Source mappings document how CRM rows, email threads, and calendar events become those entities.',
      'Keep the ontology and its referenced mappings in the persona’s versioned definitions. The workspace supplies authorized connections to the actual accounts and data.',
    ],
    link: { href: './specification/#61-ontology', text: 'Ontology definition' },
  },
  {
    id: 'use-the-mapped-data',
    phase: 'Build on the ontology',
    label: 'Apps and agents',
    title: 'Build a view, an agent, or both',
    scene: 'paths',
    body: [
      'Build a custom app to read authorized data or shared lists and show it in a graph. A prospecting dashboard could chart how many prospects are in each stage.',
      'Build agents to read the same domain data and act on it. An agent can update an existing app through a declared connector operation, or publish approved changes to the shared records a custom app reads. Both paths use the same field definitions.',
    ],
    link: { href: './concepts/records-and-apps/#two-paths-from-the-same-data', text: 'Custom views and agent updates' },
  },
  {
    id: 'goal-to-playbook',
    phase: 'Run the work',
    label: 'Team and goal',
    title: 'Give the team an outcome',
    scene: 'plan',
    body: [
      'The domain specialist sets the goal and its completion criteria. For Lane, that means qualifying Northwind with evidence and preparing a follow-up for the correct contact. The team uses registered research and outreach playbooks or drafts a missing one for review.',
      'The runtime checks the pinned definitions and binds the chosen harness and authorized connectors. The harness runs the agents on the team’s plan. The playbooks specify what each agent can read, change, and publish.',
    ],
    link: { href: './apps/#from-goals-to-playbooks', text: 'Goals and playbooks' },
  },
  {
    id: 'from-git-to-a-running-workspace',
    phase: 'Run the work',
    label: 'Specialist review',
    title: 'Ask the expert when the evidence disagrees',
    scene: 'runtime',
    body: [
      'If two Northwind records match the same domain, the agent presents both candidates and its evidence. The affected work waits while the domain specialist confirms the right record, corrects the mapping, or rejects the proposed change.',
      'An exception decision resolves the data question. A protected action still needs its own approval. Before an email goes out, the authorized reviewer sees the exact account, recipient, and message.',
    ],
    link: { href: './concepts/connectors-and-approvals/#anomalies-and-action-approvals', text: 'Anomaly review and action approvals' },
  },
  {
    id: 'worked-example',
    phase: 'Worked example',
    label: 'Write back and publish',
    title: 'See the decision reach the apps',
    scene: 'app',
    body: [
      'In this example, the specialist confirms the active Northwind record. Once the send is approved and completes, Lane can update the authorized CRM record and publish the result to the prospects list. The prospect moves to Contacted.',
      'Pilot prospects shows the new stage. A custom chart using the same list can update its stage counts as the records change. The team can trace the result to the goal, evidence, and approvals that produced it.',
    ],
    link: { href: './examples/#one-prospect-end-to-end', text: 'Follow the full Lane example' },
  },
  {
    id: 'learn-from-approvals',
    phase: 'Improve the harness',
    label: 'Learn from decisions',
    title: 'Turn expert decisions into better next runs',
    scene: 'learning',
    body: [
      'Every approval gives the team evidence about what was acceptable in that case. Keep the decision with the evidence, the reason when supplied, and the version that ran. Corrections and rejections show where a rule or mapping needs work.',
      'The team can propose an eval for companies that share a domain and a playbook change that asks for expert review when the match is ambiguous. The domain specialist reviews the proposal before an authorized owner adopts it. The harness handles more routine work while the expert stays in charge of judgment.',
    ],
    link: { href: './concepts/signals-and-evals/#learn-from-specialist-review', text: 'Learn from specialist review' },
  },
] as const;
---

<div class="hs-story not-content" data-harness-story>
  <div class="hs-chapters">
    {chapters.map((chapter) => (
      <article class="hs-chapter" id={chapter.id} data-hs-chapter>
        {'legacy' in chapter && <span id={chapter.legacy} class="hs-anchor"></span>}
        <div class="hs-copy">
          <p class="hs-phase">{chapter.phase}</p>
          <h3>{chapter.title}</h3>
          {chapter.body.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
          {'link' in chapter && <a href={chapter.link.href}>{chapter.link.text} →</a>}
        </div>
        <div class="hs-inline" data-hs-source>
          <HarnessScene scene={chapter.scene} />
        </div>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <aside class="hs-sticky" aria-label="Walkthrough diagrams" data-hs-sticky hidden>
    <div class="hs-stage">
      <div class="hs-stage-bar" aria-hidden="true">
        <span data-hs-label>{chapters[0].label}</span>
        <span data-hs-count>1 / {chapters.length}</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hs-stage-panels" data-hs-stage aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <nav class="hs-progress" aria-label="Story chapters">
        {chapters.map((chapter, index) => (
          <a href={`#${chapter.id}`} aria-label={chapter.label} title={chapter.label} data-hs-jump>
            {index + 1}
          </a>
        ))}
      </nav>
    </div>
  </aside>
</div>

<script>
  for (const story of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-harness-story]')) {
    const chapters = [...story.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-hs-chapter]')];
    const sticky = story.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hs-sticky]');
    const stage = story.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hs-stage]');
    const label = story.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hs-label]');
    const count = story.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hs-count]');
    const links = [...story.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[data-hs-jump]')];
    if (!sticky || !stage || !label || !count || !chapters.length) continue;

    const panels = chapters.map((chapter) => {
      const panel = document.createElement('div');
      panel.className = 'hs-stage-panel';
      const visual = chapter.querySelector('[data-hs-source]')?.firstElementChild;
      if (visual) panel.append(visual.cloneNode(true));
      return panel;
    });
    stage.replaceChildren(...panels);

    const wide = matchMedia('(min-width: 54rem) and (min-height: 36rem)');
    let active = -1;
    let frame = 0;

    const show = (index: number) => {
      if (active === index) return;
      active = index;
      panels.forEach((panel, i) => panel.classList.toggle('is-active', i === index));
      links.forEach((link, i) => {
        if (i === index) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'step');
        else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
      });
      label.textContent = links[index]?.getAttribute('aria-label') ?? '';
      count.textContent = `${index + 1} / ${chapters.length}`;
    };

    const measure = () => {
      const eyeLine = innerHeight * 0.54;
      let nearest = 0;
      let distance = Infinity;
      for (const [index, chapter] of chapters.entries()) {
        const { top, bottom } = chapter.getBoundingClientRect();
        const gap = Math.max(top - eyeLine, eyeLine - bottom, 0);
        if (gap < distance) {
          nearest = index;
          distance = gap;
        }
      }
      show(nearest);
    };

    const schedule = () => {
      if (!wide.matches || frame) return;
      frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        frame = 0;
        measure();
      });
    };

    const updateLayout = () => {
      story.toggleAttribute('data-hs-ready', wide.matches);
      sticky.hidden = !wide.matches;
      if (wide.matches) measure();
    };

    updateLayout();
    wide.addEventListener('change', updateLayout);
    addEventListener('scroll', schedule, { passive: true });
    addEventListener('resize', schedule);
    addEventListener('pageshow', schedule);
  }
</script>
